I tried many different strategies, but the adj.sharp varies too much accross all the assets. I believe that machine learning will be 
the most important thing in the event. 

Maybe during the event a quick machine learning or correlation can help with identifying the connection to the macro data. Also during the event i 
really want to test out the correlations and 2 different strategies with them. 1st is the idea to find the assets that are highly correlated and 
then find the periods where the correlation starts breaking, so maybe 0.1-0.2 difference and define those areas as stress conditions, so we will 
pull back risk there. 2nd is the idea of using dow theory type divergence, especially in events like 2008 or covid 
(https://www.tradingview.com/x/L7Jftzyl/). The divergence hints at a reversal, so we could increase risk there. In addition to this 
divergence idea, we could use ADX for confirmation purposes. 


* (Bullish) Traling stop loss: (Link to example: https://www.tradingview.com/x/Qv2te6GK/)

Code:
import numpy as np
import pandas as pd

def three_close_trailing_stop(close: pd.Series) -> pd.DataFrame:
    """
    3-close trailing stop (long), closes only. Exact translation of the Pine indicator.

    - 3 rising closes in a row (2 up-moves) -> stop goes under the lowest (1/3) close
    - Stop only ratchets up, never down
    - A down-close while armed -> previous close becomes the reference, ratchet suspended
    - Suspended -> re-arms after 3 rising closes in a row, all above the reference
    - Close below stop -> exit, reset, start over
    Uses only data up to each day (no look-ahead).
    """
    c = close.to_numpy(dtype=float)
    n = len(c)

    stop_out = np.full(n, np.nan)
    ref_out = np.full(n, np.nan)
    exit_out = np.zeros(n, dtype=bool)

    armed = True
    stop = np.nan
    ref = np.nan
    up_n = 0

    for i in range(n):
        # consecutive up-close counter
        up_n = up_n + 1 if i > 0 and c[i] > c[i - 1] else 0

        if not np.isnan(stop) and c[i] < stop:
            # stopped out -> reset and start over
            exit_out[i] = True
            stop = np.nan
            ref = np.nan
            armed = True
            up_n = 0
        elif armed:
            # 3 rising closes -> stop under the lowest (1/3) close
            if up_n >= 2:
                stop = c[i - 2] if np.isnan(stop) else max(stop, c[i - 2])
            # sequence break -> previous close becomes reference, ratchet suspended
            if not np.isnan(stop) and c[i] < c[i - 1]:
                armed = False
                ref = c[i - 1]
        else:
            # suspended: 3 rising closes, all above the reference -> re-arm
            if up_n >= 2 and c[i - 2] > ref:
                stop = max(stop, c[i - 2])
                armed = True
                ref = np.nan

        stop_out[i] = stop
        ref_out[i] = np.nan if armed else ref

    return pd.DataFrame(
        {"close": c, "stop": stop_out, "reference": ref_out, "exit": exit_out},
        index=close.index,
    )


